# Module Layout Rule: No Inline `mod { … }` Blocks

- Do not declare modules with an inline body in the same file (`mod foo { … }`, `pub mod foo { … }`). Use a sibling file: `foo.rs` next to the parent, or `parent/foo.rs` when the parent is already a directory module (see `no-mod-rs-modules.mdc`).
- In the parent, use `mod foo;` or `pub mod foo;` only (no braces).
- Nested inline modules are doubly disallowed (`mod a { mod b { … } }`).

## Exceptions

- **`examples/vmux/src/`:** Large single files (`log.rs`, `browser/cef.rs`, `browser/cef/osr.rs`, `browser/cef/renderer.rs`) intentionally keep many items in one module **without** nested `mod { … }`. Prefer `#[cfg(test)]` on each test helper / `#[test]` fn instead of `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { … }`.
- **Elsewhere in the repo:** `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { … }` may stay inline when splitting to `tests/` or `foo_tests.rs` is impractical. Avoid nested `mod` inside test modules unless necessary.

## Example

```rust
// ❌ BAD — inline module body
mod event {
    pub struct RuntimeState;
}

// ✅ GOOD
// runtime.rs
mod event;

// runtime/event.rs
pub struct RuntimeState;
```

```rust
// ❌ BAD — nested inline modules
mod browser {
    mod cef {
        pub fn init() {}
    }
}

// ✅ GOOD — file-based
// browser.rs
pub mod cef;

// browser/cef.rs
pub fn init() {}
```
